Here's an example implementation using the three-pointer technique:

```java
public class Solution {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr1 = { 1, 5, 10, 20, 30 };
        int[] arr2 = { 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 };
        int[] arr3 = { 2, 4, 8, 10, 12 };

        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;
        int k = 0;

        while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length && k < arr3.length) {
            if (arr1[i] == arr2[j] && arr2[j] == arr3[k]) {
                System.out.println(arr1[i]);
                i++;
                j++;
                k++;
            } else if (arr1[i] <= arr2[j] && arr2[j] <= arr3[k]) {
                j++;
            } else if (arr2[j] <= arr1[i] && arr1[i] <= arr3[k]) {
                i++;
            } else {
                k++;
            }
        }

    }
}
```

This solution has a time complexity of O(n1 + n2 + n3) and space complexity of O(1), making it more efficient than the hash map approach.
